#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198082 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e2aee319-68d6-54f7-8023-b24d32610bc4" :knowledge-point/title "Sketching Cartesian Products of Special Sets in the Coordinate Plane"}] :question/id #uuid "eae94514-cd92-5405-90b1-ee09dad18e13" :question/math-academy-id "e-13162" :question/problem "Sketch a representation of the set $S = \\mathbb{Z} \\times \\mathbb{N}$ in the Cartesian plane." :question/worked-solution "The Cartesian product $A \\times B$ consists of all ordered pairs whose first coordinate belongs to $A$ and whose second coordinate belongs to $B$.\n\n$$\nA\\times B = \\big\\{(a,b) \\::\\: a\\in A,\\, b\\in B\\big\\}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the set\n\n$$\nS = \\mathbb{Z} \\times \\mathbb{N}\n$$\n\ncontains every point in $\\mathbb{R}^2$ whose $x$-coordinate is an integer number and whose $y$-coordinate is a natural number.\n\n$$\nS= \\Big\\{(x,y) \\:: \\: x\\in \\mathbb Z,\\, y\\in \\mathbb N\\Big\\}\n$$\n\nTo visualize $S$, consider $S_1\\subset S$ containing all points whose $x$-coordinate is any integer number and whose $y$-coordinate equals ${\\color{red}{1}}$.\n\n$$\nS_1 = \\Big\\{(x, {\\color{red}{1}}) \\:: \\: x\\in \\mathbb Z\\Big\\}\n$$\n\nWe can represent $S_1$ in the plane as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/4387/Source/Images/8f5aa73042b1d2acceb1bb3905a19383.png)\n\nNext, consider $S_2\\subset S$ containing all points whose $x$-coordinate is any integer number and whose $y$-coordinate equals ${\\color{blue}{2}}$.\n\n$$\nS_2 = \\Big\\{(x,{\\color{blue}{2}}) \\:: \\: x\\in \\mathbb Z\\Big\\}\n$$\n\nWe can represent $S_2$ in the plane as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/4387/Source/Images/61f4160522933e19954c880409db96e5.png)\n\nWe can create the subsets $S_3,S_4,S_5,\\ldots$, similarly. Our Cartesian product $S$ is the *union* of these subsets:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nS &= S_{1}∪S_{2}∪S_{3}∪S_{4}∪… \\\\\n&= ⋃_(i ∈ N)S_{i}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can visualize $\\mathbb{Z} \\times \\mathbb{N}$ as the set of points in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/4387/Source/Images/0f3b930634c622930def542df33bddf4.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198097 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c3a2b291-c6dd-58c8-a10f-d851511d7bfc" :knowledge-point/title "Sketching Cartesian Products in the Coordinate Plane"}] :question/id #uuid "bb40c174-3739-50df-835e-5d47c9902d5b" :question/math-academy-id "e-13192" :question/problem "Sketch a representation of the set $S = \\{1, 2\\} \\times (0, 1)$ in the Cartesian plane." :question/worked-solution "The Cartesian product $A \\times B$ consists of all ordered pairs whose first coordinate belongs to $A$ and whose second coordinate belongs to $B$.\n\n$$\nA\\times B = \\big\\{(a,b) \\::\\: a\\in A,\\, b\\in B\\big\\}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the set\n\n$$\nS = \\{1,2\\} \\times (0,1)\n$$\n\ncontains every point in $\\mathbb{R}^2$ whose $x$-coordinate is either $1$ or $2$ and whose $y$-coordinate is any real number in the interval $(0,1)$.\n\n$$\nS= \\Big\\{(x,y) \\:: \\: x\\in \\{{\\color{red}{1}},{\\color{blue}{2}}\\},\\, y\\in (0,1) \\Big\\}\n$$\n\nTo visualize $S$, consider $S_1\\subset S$ containing all points whose $x$-coordinate equals ${\\color{red}{1}}$ and whose $y$-coordinate is any real number in the interval $(0,1)$.\n\n$$\nS_1 = \\Big\\{({\\color{red}{1}},y) \\:: \\: y \\in(0,1) \\Big\\}\n$$\n\nThis set is represented by the line segment shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/4387/Source/Images/3b80da3b101386f2d3a40a0bf941e20d.png)\n\nNext, consider $S_2\\subset S$ containing all points whose $x$-coordinate equals ${\\color{blue}{2}}$ and whose $y$-coordinate is any real number in the interval $(0,1)$.\n\n$$\nS_2 = \\big\\{({\\color{blue}{2}},y) \\:: \\: y \\in(0,1) \\big\\}\n$$\n\nThis set is represented by the line segment shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/4387/Source/Images/b5e4c38cb65a7cd28c8770c6ffe1f66d.png)\n\nOur Cartesian product is the *union* of these subsets:\n\n$$\nS = S_1\\cup S_2\n$$\n\nTherefore, $S$ can be represented by the two line segments shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/4387/Source/Images/01bdb54466facecd2edb472318568c45.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198133 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6290e701-b4ea-5a86-ab7e-7371c5bdc11e" :knowledge-point/title "Visualizing Unions of Uncountable Families of Indexed Sets"}] :question/id #uuid "aa337681-5d8c-5ea1-b0fe-5d8cd15040a7" :question/math-academy-id "e-13320" :question/problem "Sketch a representation of the set $\\,\\displaystyle \\bigcup_{i \\in I} X_i$ in the Cartesian plane given that $I = [-1,1] \\subset \\mathbb{R}$ and $X_i = \\{i \\} \\times [0, \\, i^2]$." :question/worked-solution "Recall that the union of a family of indexed sets consists of those elements that belong to *at least one* set:\n\n$$\n\\bigcup_{i \\in I} A_i = \\big\\{x \\:: \\: x \\in A_k \\:\\:\\textrm{for at least one}\\:\\: k \\in I \\big\\}\n$$\n\nLet's now write down one of our indexed sets. For example, if $i=-0.5$, we have\n\n$$\nX_{-0.5} = \\{-0.5 \\} \\times [0, 0.25] = \\big\\{(-0.5,k) \\:: \\: k \\in [0, 0.25] \\big\\}\n$$\n\nThis represents a vertical line segment from the point $(-0.5, 0)$ to the point $(-0.5,0.25)$ in the coordinate plane, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/4387/Source/Images/39d7728d4b077bbadefe0f4e78ccdac8.png)\n\nIn general,\n\n$$\nX_i = \\{i \\} \\times [0, i^2] = \\big\\{(i,k) \\:: \\: k \\in [0, i^2] \\big\\}\n$$\n\nis the vertical line segment from the point $(i,0)$ to the point $(i, i^2)$.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/4387/Source/Images/8b4e8f9f69ee8255a65b8604ebb5d208.png)\n\nAs $i$ runs over $I$, these line segments sweep the following figure that represents the union $\\displaystyle \\bigcup_{i \\in I} X_i{:}$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/4387/Source/Images/a61ed7144cb09c3bb8d7f03cae547e73.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198134 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5793f07d-e0de-5707-a5e1-32d657244e0b" :knowledge-point/title "Visualizing Intersections of Uncountable Families of Indexed Sets"}] :question/id #uuid "67569060-2f33-5a8d-ae73-690742d3d318" :question/math-academy-id "e-13321" :question/problem "Let $I = [0,1] \\subset \\mathbb{R}$ and $X_i = [-i, 0] \\times [-1,1]$. Sketch a representation of $\\displaystyle \\bigcap_{i \\in I} X_i$ in the Cartesian plane." :question/worked-solution "Recall that the intersection of a family of indexed sets consists of those elements that belong to *all* the sets:\n\n$$\n\\bigcap_{i \\in I} A_i = \\big\\{x \\:: \\: x \\in A_k \\:\\:\\textrm{for all}\\:\\: k \\in I \\big\\}\n$$\n\nLet's now write down one of our indexed sets. For example, if $i=0.5$, we have\n\n$$\nX_{0.5} = [-0.5,0] \\times [-1, 1] = \\big\\{(a,b) \\:: \\: a \\in [-0.5, 0], b \\in [-1, 1] \\big\\}\n$$\n\nThis represents a rectangle whose vertices are at $(-0.5, -1)$, $(-0.5, 1)$, $(0, 1)$, and $(0, -1)$ in the coordinate plane, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/4387/Source/Images/ddb9d22d2183ec9959a2da585dedfc81.png)\n\nIn general,\n\n$$\nX_i = [-i, 0] \\times [-1,1] = \\big\\{(a,b) \\:: \\: a \\in [-i, 0], b \\in [-1, 1] \\big\\}\n$$\n\nis the rectangle whose vertices are at $(-i,-1)$, $(-i,1)$, $(0, 1)$, and $(0, -1)$.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/4387/Source/Images/64febcdf1b6f8c3dd38c61e6e1269688.png)\n\nThe intersection of all these rectangles is the line segment $\\{0\\}\\times [-1,1]{:}$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/4387/Source/Images/58f000d1c247eea66c409933d93cf088.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198644 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5793f07d-e0de-5707-a5e1-32d657244e0b" :knowledge-point/title "Visualizing Intersections of Uncountable Families of Indexed Sets"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bdc3da06-8f00-598b-8347-6e0df5009fe9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/4387/Source/Images/q-172690-a-4.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186194761} {:answer/id #uuid "bee459c5-4bea-53dc-8207-61722752c483" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$∅$, the empty set" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186194762} {:answer/id #uuid "d4d7758f-c860-50e2-b757-40cef4b3ed8b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/4387/Source/Images/q-172690-a-5.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186194763} {:answer/id #uuid "d8e28058-2009-5cf8-b079-f46f55015e2d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/4387/Source/Images/q-172690-a-2.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186194764} {:answer/id #uuid "e5378d60-106a-59d0-add8-0310c7052468" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/4387/Source/Images/q-172690-a-3.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186194765}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bee459c5-4bea-53dc-8207-61722752c483" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$∅$, the empty set" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186194762} :answer-field/id #uuid "7e5694af-5147-5d01-be63-50cda1092e49" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186194760}] :question/id #uuid "30b7b0c8-79fe-53ad-bb30-601d2f7e078d" :question/math-academy-id "q-172690" :question/problem "Let $I = [0, 2]⊂R$ and $X_{i} = {i} \\cdot [0, i]$. Which of the following represents $⋂_(i ∈ I)X_{i}$?"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198705 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e2aee319-68d6-54f7-8023-b24d32610bc4" :knowledge-point/title "Sketching Cartesian Products of Special Sets in the Coordinate Plane"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "957afc7c-bb56-510f-8194-063b82